\section{Theoretical Framework}
\label{sec:theory}

In this work we develop a cosmological model in which spacetime geometry,
gravitational curvature, and early supermassive black hole (SMBH) formation
emerge from the dynamics of a Primordial Informational Field (PIF). The PIF
constitutes the fundamental substrate of the theory, with informational density
$\rho_I$ and flow velocity $\mathbf{v}_I$ serving as the primary dynamical
variables. Conventional geometric quantities, including the metric tensor
$g_{\mu\nu}$ and curvature invariants, arise as coarse-grained functionals of
informational gradients.

The evolution of the PIF is governed by the Informational Field Equation (IFE),
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \rho_I + \nabla \cdot (\rho_I \mathbf{v}_I)
= \alpha (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}_I) + \beta \rho_I^2,
\label{eq:ife}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ encode non-linear transport properties of the
informational substrate. Equation~(\ref{eq:ife}) generalizes classical
continuity relations by incorporating a quadratic informational source term,
reflecting the self-interacting nature of the PIF.

Spacetime geometry emerges from informational gradients through the relation
\begin{equation}
g_{\mu\nu} = g^{(0)}_{\mu\nu}
+ A\,\partial_\mu \partial_\nu \rho_I
+ B\,(\partial_\mu \rho_I)(\partial_\nu \rho_I),
\label{eq:eme}
\end{equation}
where $g^{(0)}_{\mu\nu}$ is a background metric and $(A,B)$ are coefficients
determined by the UV-regularized quadratic gravity action. Equation~(\ref{eq:eme})
implies that curvature invariants are functionals of informational gradients,
leading to the curvature--information duality:
\begin{equation}
R = c_1 \Box \rho_I + c_2 (\partial \rho_I)^2,
\label{eq:ricci}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
C^2 = d_1 (\partial \partial \rho_I)^2 + d_2 (\partial \rho_I)^4,
\label{eq:weyl}
\end{equation}
where $R$ is the Ricci scalar and $C^2$ is the Weyl curvature invariant.
Equations~(\ref{eq:ricci})--(\ref{eq:weyl}) demonstrate that high-curvature
regimes correspond to regions of rapidly varying informational density, rather
than geometric singularities.

Black holes are modeled as informational sinks. Their growth is governed by the
SMBH Informational Sink Equation (SISE),
\begin{equation}
\dot{M}_{\rm BH} = \gamma\,\rho_I\,A_0(1+\delta),
\label{eq:sise}
\end{equation}
where $\gamma$ is the informational-to-mass conversion coefficient, $A_0$ is the
baseline capture area, and $\delta$ quantifies chaotic inflow amplification.
Integrating Eq.~(\ref{eq:sise}) yields
\begin{equation}
M_{\rm BH}(t) = M_{\rm seed} 
+ \gamma A_0 (1+\delta) \int_{t_0}^{t} \rho_I(t')\,dt',
\label{eq:sise_int}
\end{equation}
indicating that SMBH mass is the time-integral of local informational density.
This mechanism naturally produces $10^9\,M_\odot$ SMBHs by $z>7$, consistent
with JWST observations.

The combined system defined by Eqs.~(\ref{eq:ife})--(\ref{eq:sise_int}) forms a
self-consistent informational cosmology in which geometry, curvature, and
structure formation arise from informational dynamics. Numerical simulations
performed with QIA-SE v9.4 confirm stability under extreme curvature, 
non-linear informational gradients, and super-Eddington accretion, establishing
the viability of the framework.